Let 10-bit software video use R16 planes on OHOS when the GPU has norm16.

VideoResourceUpdater uploads a software-decoded 10-bit frame as three
planes: R16 when the shared image interface allows R16 images and the
context has texture_norm16, half-float otherwise. Upstream allows R16
shared images only with ANGLE Metal or Skia Graphite, a restriction its own
TODO calls possibly too pessimistic (crbug.com/481590672), so this port on
Skia GL always took half-float.

ohos-gpu-stage-diagnostics.patch showed what that costs on a Mate 70 Pro+
(Maleoon, GLES through ANGLE): RasterDecoderImpl::DoWritePixelsYUVINTERNAL
took 5-6 ms for the half-float planes of one 1080p frame, about three times
what their size explains against under 1 ms for an 8-bit frame, and viz
could not acknowledge the video frame until the write was done. A third of
YouTube's 1080p60 HDR frames dropped in the default power mode.

On OHOS SharedImageFactory also allows R16 shared images on Skia GL when the
context has GL_EXT_texture_norm16 and Skia can sample R16 images, and logs
once whether the extension, Skia's support and the result are there ("OHOS
shared image caps"). The frame's 10 bits are then shifted to the top of 16
on the CPU instead of converted to half-float.

Generated against Chromium 154.0.8037.51, on top of chromium-154-harmonyos.patch.

Apply with:
  git -C /path/to/chromium/src apply /path/to/patches/ohos-video-r16-planes.patch

